---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import XraySpectrum from "../components/calc/XraySpectrum.tsx";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import { EX } from "../data/worked-examples";
import { FIELD_PRO } from "../lib/tools";
import { u } from "../lib/site";
const D = EX.xray;
---
<ToolPage slug="xray-spectrum">
  <XraySpectrum slot="tool" client:only="react" />
  <Fragment slot="prose">
    <!-- ★ 2026-10-26 감독 지시 20: 소유주가 이 쪽을 보고 「여전히 간이 도구뿐」 — 여과 뒤 선질은
         머리글 메뉴에만 있었다. 산문 맨 앞, 도구 카드와 떨어진 자리에 안내를 둔다.
         바탕은 Result 칸과 같은 강조 바탕, 단추는 바탕색 `.btn` 이라 바탕 위에서 갈린다.
         h2 를 쓰지 않는다 — 산문 h2 는 번호가 붙는다(「1. The continuous spectrum」 유지). -->
    <aside aria-labelledby="pro-callout-h"
      class="not-prose rounded-lg border border-accent/40 bg-accent-soft p-4 text-[14px] sm:p-5">
      <p id="pro-callout-h" class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
        Need filtration, HVL and ISO 4037 matching?
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        This calculator covers only the unfiltered bremsstrahlung spectrum and its mean energy.
        Inherent and added filtration, the first and second half-value layers, ISO 4037 beam-quality
        matching, Cs-137 and Co-60 fields, operational dose conversion coefficients, reading
        corrections and uncertainty are worked out in Radiation Field Pro, the sister workbench on
        this platform.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2">
        <a href={FIELD_PRO} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn font-semibold">
          Open Radiation Field Pro ↗
        </a>
        <a href={u("/iso-4037-reference-fields/")} class="underline">What Field Pro computes</a>
      </p>
    </aside>

    <h2>The continuous spectrum</h2>
    <p>
      When fast electrons strike the tungsten anode, most lose energy gradually and radiate a
      continuous band of X-rays — bremsstrahlung, or "braking radiation". The highest photon energy
      equals the tube voltage: a 100&nbsp;kVp tube can make a 100&nbsp;keV photon and no more. Raise
      the voltage and the whole curve extends to the right and grows; the mean energy climbs with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This view shows the raw spectrum leaving the anode, before any filter. It is soft and
      low-energy: in practice the tube housing and an added aluminium or copper filter remove the
      lowest energies first, and those filters are outside what this calculator draws.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the chart</h2>
    <p>
      The horizontal axis is photon energy in keV, from zero to the chosen tube voltage, so the
      right-hand edge of the chart is always the endpoint. The vertical axis is relative photon
      fluence per keV, scaled so that the tallest point of the current spectrum is 1. The chart therefore compares <em>shapes</em>, not output: it does not say how many more
      photons a higher voltage produces, only where along the energy axis they fall.
    </p>
    <p>
      The log-scale option spreads the vertical axis over several decades. Use it to see the
      high-energy tail near the endpoint, which on a linear axis is squeezed against the baseline,
      and to check that the curve reaches zero exactly at the tube voltage.
    </p>
    <p>
      The narrow peak just below 10&nbsp;keV, and the sharp fall after it, are part of the model, not
      a drawing error. Photons made inside the anode must cross some tungsten to escape, and tungsten
      absorbs much more strongly just above its L-shell edges at about 10.2, 11.5 and 12.1&nbsp;keV.
      The smaller step near 2&ndash;3&nbsp;keV comes from the M-shell edges in the same way. The glass or
      metal envelope of a diagnostic tube, and any added filter, absorb almost all of this region,
      which is why measured spectra from such tubes do not show it.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the raw spectrum is soft</h2>
    <p>
      In a thick target, the number of photons produced per keV rises steadily towards low
      energies — the simple form of this is Kramers' rule. On their way out of the anode, however,
      the lowest-energy photons are absorbed by the tungsten itself, so the curve turns over and
      falls away below about 10&nbsp;keV instead of rising without limit. What remains is a broad
      hump near the low end with a long, thinning tail out to the endpoint.
    </p>
    <p>The model behind this calculator gives these figures for the unfiltered tungsten beam:</p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Tube voltage</th>
            <th class="text-right">Mean photon energy</th>
            <th class="text-right">Photons below 20&nbsp;keV</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">50&nbsp;kVp</td><td class="num text-right">15.6&nbsp;keV</td><td class="num text-right">69%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">80&nbsp;kVp</td><td class="num text-right">24.4&nbsp;keV</td><td class="num text-right">47%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">100&nbsp;kVp</td><td class="num text-right">30.0&nbsp;keV</td><td class="num text-right">37%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">150&nbsp;kVp</td><td class="num text-right">43.2&nbsp;keV</td><td class="num text-right">22%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Across this range the mean energy of the raw beam stays close to 30% of the tube voltage. A
      large share of the photons sit below 20&nbsp;keV; in a patient almost all of these are
      absorbed in the first few centimetres of tissue, adding to skin dose without reaching the
      image receptor. That is the reason every diagnostic tube carries filtration — general
      radiography typically requires a total of at least 2.5&nbsp;mm of aluminium equivalent.
    </p>

    <h2>Using it</h2>
    <p>
      Move the voltage slider and watch the endpoint and the mean energy move together. Going from
      60 to 120&nbsp;kVp roughly doubles the mean energy, while the low-energy hump hardly moves:
      most of the change happens in the tail. This is a quick way to show why a higher voltage
      penetrates further, and why the voltage alone does not fix beam quality once a filter is
      added.
    </p>

    <h3>What this view leaves out</h3>
    <p>
      Characteristic lines (the sharp spikes near 58–69&nbsp;keV that appear once the tube voltage
      passes tungsten's K-edge at 69.5&nbsp;kV) and filtration are not part of this calculator, so it gives no half-value layer or air kerma.
      The source is treated as a point on the central axis, so there is no heel effect here. For a
      filtered beam, use a full spectrum model such as SpekPy, which this calculator's table is
      drawn from.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked examples</h2>
    <h3>Raising the tube voltage from 70 to 90 kVp</h3>
    <p>
      A radiographer raises an exposure from 70&nbsp;kVp to 90&nbsp;kVp to get through a thicker
      patient. Set the slider to each value and read the mean energy under the chart. The two
      fractions in the table are summed from the same spectrum the chart draws.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Quantity</th><th class="text-right">70 kVp</th><th class="text-right">90 kVp</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Mean energy, keV</td><td class="num text-right">{D.m70}</td><td class="num text-right">{D.m90}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Photons below 20 keV</td><td class="num text-right">{D.below20at70}%</td><td class="num text-right">{D.below20at90}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Photons at 50 keV or more</td><td class="num text-right">{D.above50at70}%</td><td class="num text-right">{D.above50at90}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The mean energy rises by about a quarter, but the share of photons at 50&nbsp;keV or more more
      than doubles, and those are the photons that reach the image receptor through tissue. The
      low-energy share falls too, yet it remains large in the raw beam. In a real tube most of it is
      removed by the inherent and added filtration before it reaches the patient, which is exactly the
      part this view does not model, so read the table as a comparison of shapes rather than of patient
      dose.
    </p>

    <h3>A dental unit next to a CT scanner</h3>
    <p>
      The same calculator can show why survey work around different X-ray units needs different
      care. Compare 60&nbsp;kVp, typical of an intraoral dental unit, with 120&nbsp;kVp, typical of CT.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Quantity</th><th class="text-right">60 kVp</th><th class="text-right">120 kVp</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Mean energy, keV</td><td class="num text-right">{D.m60}</td><td class="num text-right">{D.m120}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Photons at 40 keV or more</td><td class="num text-right">{D.above40at60}%</td><td class="num text-right">{D.above40at120}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Most common energy, keV</td><td class="num text-right">{D.peakKeV}</td><td class="num text-right">{D.peakKeV}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Doubling the voltage nearly doubles the mean energy and multiplies the share above 40&nbsp;keV
      by more than four, while the most common photon energy in the raw beam stays at the same place,
      just below tungsten's L-shell edges. Scattered radiation around both units is softer still. A
      survey meter calibrated with Cs-137 at 662&nbsp;keV can read well off at these energies, so check
      its energy response for the range shown here, or use an ionisation chamber with a flat response
      down to the energies that matter.
    </p>

    <Faq slug="xray-spectrum" />
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
